/**
 * A moment is a `float`: seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC, with a fraction. Reading the clock is
 * an effect (`now`, `timed`); turning a moment into text and back is pure and always in UTC, so the
 * same moment gives the same text on every machine.
 */

/// The current time, in seconds since the epoch.
/// @example now()    // 1791806400.25
export func now(): float uses clock

/// Runs f(x) once and gives its result together with the seconds it took, measured on a monotonic
/// clock. Timing is made a call of its own because the reading has to be about this very run of
/// f: the call is not computed ahead, moved or merged with another, and on a device it waits for
/// the work to finish. Two `now()` with a pure computation between them can be folded or moved,
/// and measure nothing. The call uses the clock and whatever f uses.
/// @example timed((x: int): int => x * 2, 21).0    // 42
export func timed::<T, R>(f: (x: T) => R, x: T): (R, float) uses clock

/// The moment written with a strftime format, in UTC.
/// @fails fault `TimeFormatFailed` when the moment is outside the range of calendar time, or the format gives no text
/// @example 0.0->strftime<'%Y-%m-%d'>    // '1970-01-01'
with <format: string>
export func strftime(timestamp: float): string pure

/// The moment a text gives when read with a strptime format; the fields are taken as UTC.
/// @fails error value `ParseError` when the text does not have the format, or gives a moment outside the range of calendar time; fault `TimeFormatFailed` when the format itself cannot be used
/// @example '1970-01-02'->strptime<'%Y-%m-%d'> !! 0.0    // 86400.0
with <format: string>
export func strptime(text: string): float! pure
